{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for journal-floor draft5 at exact6342addcdd51c79da59d4437e97c48ea2a230dc7, tree1babfb3a60e2a446566ecd05c0a90a9d901868df. Primary63d240eb was read FIRST IN FULL (702lines); whole delta from149e50ce reviewed. One remaining P2 finding in the newly added binding lifecycle.\n\n[P2] Restrict a new binding to first initialization, or specify a bounded safe replacement. Writer rule9 at lines164-165 says a new archive binding starts from0 and sets every retained row's `pass` to null. For a replacement binding after N confirmed lifetime keys, that transition updates N rows. The retained tables keep those keys for the scope's life; neither the512-per-pass limit nor the floor bounds N. The rule gives this reset no bounded batch, budget charge or refusal. It therefore puts history-dependent whole-store work into maintenance of the new locator, despite the bounded lookup/confirmation guarantee. The new restart witness only exercises an unchanged binding.\n\nThere is also a correctness constraint on that same replacement transition: after a floor F\u003e0 has deleted an unkept entry belowF, starting a new target again at0 cannot read that entry locally. The previous floor receipt still names a commit in the old archive; clearing locators and changing the binding does not copy its history or preserve access to that receipt in the replacement repository. No replacement/migration rule supplies it.\n\nThe declared baseline currently refuses a changed repository or format for an existing ArchiveRow (`archive-row.ts:110-119`, especially116). If 'new binding' means only the first-ever binding, state that restriction, keep the existing conflict behavior and initialize null locators without a lifetime-table reset. That is a narrow solution within this design. If replacement is intended (surveyda2fbba4 calls this rebinding), it needs an explicit separately bounded migration: locator invalidation tied to binding identity, charged bounded updates/restart behavior, and preservation of the old archived prefix and floor receipt. Extend the witness with the supported binding transition after many keys and, if replacement remains allowed, after a floor. This is a static protocol/maintenance gap, not an observed runtime failure.\n\nThe ordinary-pass portion of1c83e97a is fixed. Exact kind/digest or domain/digest primary keys now locate the owning confirmed pass with at most512 charged metadata reads and no search through historical manifests/trees. Confirmation sets only the at-most512 written keys in the same store transaction as the receipt; abandonment publishes no locator; v1 conversion assigns pass0; restart preserves row and receipt together. The existing tree-work witness now reuses a nonrecent3700 key and a new key after restart, and conversion checks reuse of pass0. Native retained schemas, confirmation and redaction citations support these choices: archived kinds exclude text, while the sole current retained deletion is text redaction. The locator is bookkeeping outside state/checkpoint exports.\n\nAll earlier repaired floor findings remain addressed in the unchanged text, including completed OwnRequest hashes, stable per-pass paths and numeric directory limits. The new delta is confined to the requested owner index, its maintenance/citations and the existing tree-work/conversion witnesses. All2569 other tracked paths remain byte/blob/mode identical to draft4 and the declared7a148325 baseline. Eleven exact baseline source files were preserved, with candidate/baseline blob identity checked; changed citations were inspected in the native retained schemas/read/retain/forget path, turn redaction, ArchiveRows target/confirmation, archive driver, path kinds and archive budget. Existing target immutability is why the distinction above matters. No floor/v2 program, test-host archive or software test was executed; this remains design-only.\n\nNative Source63d240eb is effective/current at exact head/path/directbb6edf